Oracle DBA Lab — Users, Tablespaces & Privileges
Lab Objective

This exercise teaches:

Creating a tablespace
Creating users
Assigning default tablespaces
Understanding DBA_USERS
CONNECT privilege
RESOURCE privilege
UNLIMITED TABLESPACE
Creating tables
Object privileges
GRANT SELECT
Schema-qualified table access
System privileges
WITH ADMIN OPTION
Privilege propagation
REVOKE
Understanding why a revoked privilege may remain with a grantee
Q1. Connect to ORCL / DEMO / Available Database
Terminal
C:\Users\Administrator>sqlplus / as sysdba

SQL*Plus: Release 19.0.0.0.0 - Production
...

SQL> SHOW USER;
USER is "SYS"
Understand

We connect as SYS because creating tablespaces and users requires administrative privileges.

Q2. Create APPS_TBS of Size 10M

The datafile must be named D1.DBF.

SQL> CREATE TABLESPACE apps_tbs
  2  DATAFILE 'D:\LABS\D1.DBF'
  3  SIZE 10M;

Tablespace created.
Verify
SQL> SELECT tablespace_name, file_name
  2  FROM dba_data_files
  3  WHERE tablespace_name = 'APPS_TBS';

TABLESPACE_NAME
------------------------------
FILE_NAME
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
APPS_TBS
D:\LABS\D1.DBF
Understand
APPS_TBS = logical tablespace
D1.DBF   = physical datafile
10M      = initial size
Q3. Create User GUEST

Assign APPS_TBS as the default tablespace.

SQL> CREATE USER guest
  2  IDENTIFIED BY oracle
  3  DEFAULT TABLESPACE apps_tbs;

User created.
Understand

At this point:

GUEST
  |
  └── Default Tablespace
          |
          └── APPS_TBS

But GUEST cannot necessarily log in yet.

Q4. Verify GUEST from DBA_USERS

This must be done as SYS/privileged user.

SQL> SELECT username, default_tablespace
  2  FROM dba_users
  3  WHERE username = 'GUEST';

USERNAME
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DEFAULT_TABLESPACE
------------------------------
GUEST
APPS_TBS
Understand

The Data Dictionary confirms:

GUEST → APPS_TBS
Q5. Connect as GUEST
SQL> CONNECT guest/oracle
Expected initially

You may receive:

ERROR:
ORA-01045: user GUEST lacks CREATE SESSION privilege; logon denied
Why?

Creating a user does not automatically mean that the user can log in.

GUEST needs:

CREATE SESSION
Q6. Grant CONNECT, RESOURCE & UNLIMITED TABLESPACE

Connect back as SYS:

SQL> CONNECT / AS SYSDBA
Connected.

Then:

SQL> GRANT CONNECT, RESOURCE, UNLIMITED TABLESPACE TO guest;

Grant succeeded.
Understand

The lab's hint is:

GRANT CONNECT, RESOURCE, UNLIMITED TABLESPACE TO guest;

Conceptually:

CONNECT
   ↓
Allows session/login

RESOURCE
   ↓
Provides commonly used object-creation privileges

UNLIMITED TABLESPACE
   ↓
No tablespace quota restriction for the user
Q7. Check GUEST Connection
SQL> CONNECT guest/oracle
Connected.

SQL> SHOW USER;
USER is "GUEST"

Now GUEST can log in.

Q8. Create EMP Under GUEST

This is an important step.

You are now:

USER = GUEST

Create the table:

SQL> CREATE TABLE emp (
  2      code NUMBER,
  3      name VARCHAR2(50)
  4  );

Table created.
Verify
SQL> SELECT table_name
  2  FROM user_tables;

TABLE_NAME
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
EMP

Insert records:

SQL> INSERT INTO emp VALUES (1, 'Oracle');

1 row created.

SQL> INSERT INTO emp VALUES (2, 'Database');

1 row created.

SQL> INSERT INTO emp VALUES (3, 'Tablespace');

1 row created.

SQL> COMMIT;

Commit complete.

Check:

SQL> SELECT * FROM emp;

      CODE NAME
---------- --------------------------------------------------
         1 Oracle
         2 Database
         3 Tablespace
Understand

Now the important object exists:

GUEST schema
     |
     └── EMP
          ├── CODE
          └── NAME
Q9. Create DEV_USR

Connect as SYS:

SQL> CONNECT / AS SYSDBA
Connected.

Create the user:

SQL> CREATE USER dev_usr
  2  IDENTIFIED BY oracle
  3  DEFAULT TABLESPACE apps_tbs;

User created.
Q10. Repeat Q4, Q5 & Q6 for DEV_USR
Q10A — Check DEV_USR
SQL> SELECT username, default_tablespace
  2  FROM dba_users
  3  WHERE username = 'DEV_USR';

USERNAME
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DEFAULT_TABLESPACE
------------------------------
DEV_USR
APPS_TBS
Q10B — Try connecting
SQL> CONNECT dev_usr/oracle

Initially, login should fail because DEV_USR has not yet been granted CREATE SESSION.

Q10C — Grant privileges

Connect as SYS:

SQL> CONNECT / AS SYSDBA
Connected.

SQL> GRANT CONNECT, RESOURCE, UNLIMITED TABLESPACE TO dev_usr;

Grant succeeded.

Now:

SQL> CONNECT dev_usr/oracle
Connected.

SQL> SHOW USER;
USER is "DEV_USR"
Q11. GUEST Grants SELECT on EMP to DEV_USR

This is where you previously got:

ORA-00942

because GUEST.EMP had not yet been created.

Now it exists.

Connect as GUEST:

SQL> CONNECT guest/oracle
Connected.

SQL> SHOW USER;
USER is "GUEST"

Grant:

SQL> GRANT SELECT ON emp TO dev_usr;

Grant succeeded.
Understand

The privilege flow is:

GUEST
  |
  └── EMP
       |
       | SELECT privilege
       ↓
    DEV_USR
Q12. DEV_USR Queries GUEST.EMP

Connect as DEV_USR:

SQL> CONNECT dev_usr/oracle
Connected.

SQL> SELECT * FROM guest.emp;

      CODE NAME
---------- --------------------------------------------------
         1 Oracle
         2 Database
         3 Tablespace
Why guest.emp?

Because EMP belongs to GUEST.

guest.emp
   ↑
schema.table

If you simply write:

SELECT * FROM emp;

Oracle looks for:

DEV_USR.EMP

because the current user is DEV_USR.

Q13. DEV_USR Creates Another EMP

You are already DEV_USR.

SQL> CREATE TABLE emp (
  2      c1 NUMBER,
  3      c2 VARCHAR2(50),
  4      c3 VARCHAR2(50)
  5  );

Table created.
Important concept

Now there are two EMP tables:

GUEST
 └── EMP
      CODE
      NAME

DEV_USR
 └── EMP
      C1
      C2
      C3

Same table name, different schemas.

Q14. Insert Records into DEV_USR.EMP
SQL> INSERT INTO emp VALUES (1, 'A', 'Oracle');

1 row created.

SQL> INSERT INTO emp VALUES (2, 'B', 'Database');

1 row created.

SQL> INSERT INTO emp VALUES (3, 'C', 'Admin');

1 row created.

SQL> COMMIT;

Commit complete.

Check:

SQL> SELECT * FROM emp;

        C1 C2       C3
---------- -------- ------------------
         1 A        Oracle
         2 B        Database
         3 C        Admin
Q15. DEV_USR Grants SELECT on EMP to HR

Because DEV_USR owns this EMP, remain connected as DEV_USR.

SQL> SHOW USER;
USER is "DEV_USR"

Then:

SQL> GRANT SELECT ON emp TO hr;

Grant succeeded.
Understand
DEV_USR
   |
   └── EMP
        |
        | SELECT
        ↓
       HR
Q16. HR Checks the Privilege

Connect as HR.

If you don't know the HR password, as SYS:

SQL> CONNECT / AS SYSDBA
Connected.

SQL> ALTER USER hr IDENTIFIED BY oracle;

User altered.

Then:

SQL> CONNECT hr/oracle
Connected.

SQL> SHOW USER;
USER is "HR"

Query:

SQL> SELECT * FROM dev_usr.emp;

        C1 C2       C3
---------- -------- ------------------
         1 A        Oracle
         2 B        Database
         3 C        Admin
Understand

HR can access:

DEV_USR.EMP

because DEV_USR granted SELECT to HR.

Q17. SYS Grants CREATE USER to HR WITH ADMIN OPTION

Connect as SYS:

SQL> CONNECT / AS SYSDBA
Connected.

Run:

SQL> GRANT CREATE USER TO hr WITH ADMIN OPTION;

Grant succeeded.
Important

CREATE USER is a system privilege.

For system privileges:

WITH ADMIN OPTION

means the recipient can grant that system privilege to another user.

Q18. HR Grants CREATE USER to DEV_USR

Connect as HR:

SQL> CONNECT hr/oracle
Connected.

SQL> SHOW USER;
USER is "HR"

Then:

SQL> GRANT CREATE USER TO dev_usr;

Grant succeeded.

Privilege chain:

SYS
 │
 │ CREATE USER
 │ WITH ADMIN OPTION
 ↓
HR
 │
 │ CREATE USER
 ↓
DEV_USR
Q19. Check Whether DEV_USR Can Create a User

Connect:

SQL> CONNECT dev_usr/oracle
Connected.

Try:

SQL> CREATE USER testuser IDENTIFIED BY oracle;

User created.

Therefore:

DEV_USR has CREATE USER
Q20. SYS Revokes CREATE USER From HR

Connect as SYS:

SQL> CONNECT / AS SYSDBA
Connected.

Run:

SQL> REVOKE CREATE USER FROM hr;

Revoke succeeded.

HR no longer has the privilege.

Q21. DEV_USR Creates USERNEW

Connect:

SQL> CONNECT dev_usr/oracle
Connected.

Now:

SQL> CREATE USER usernew IDENTIFIED BY oracle;

User created.
Observation

This is the important result of the exercise.

Even though SYS revoked CREATE USER from HR, the CREATE USER privilege that HR had already granted to DEV_USR remains with DEV_USR.

Q22. Final Observation

Write this in your lab record:

Observation: SYS granted the CREATE USER system privilege to HR with ADMIN OPTION. Therefore, HR was authorized to grant the same system privilege to DEV_USR. After SYS revoked CREATE USER from HR, the privilege previously granted by HR to DEV_USR was not automatically revoked. DEV_USR therefore continued to possess the CREATE USER privilege and was able to create USERNEW.

⭐ Most Important Concepts From This Lab
1. Schema vs Tablespace
SCHEMA
   ↓
Owns database objects

TABLESPACE
   ↓
Provides logical storage

Example:

GUEST
  |
  └── EMP
       |
       └── stored in APPS_TBS
2. Same Table Name Can Exist in Different Schemas
GUEST.EMP
       ↓
CODE, NAME

DEV_USR.EMP
       ↓
C1, C2, C3

Therefore:

SELECT * FROM emp;

means:

current_user.emp

while:

SELECT * FROM guest.emp;

explicitly means:

GUEST.EMP
3. Object Privilege

Example:

GRANT SELECT ON emp TO dev_usr;

This is an object privilege.

Concept:

GUEST owns EMP
        ↓
GUEST grants SELECT
        ↓
DEV_USR can SELECT
4. System Privilege

Example:

GRANT CREATE USER TO hr WITH ADMIN OPTION;

CREATE USER is a system privilege.

5. ADMIN OPTION
GRANT CREATE USER TO hr WITH ADMIN OPTION;

means:

HR can use CREATE USER
+
HR can grant CREATE USER to another user
6. GRANT OPTION vs ADMIN OPTION

Remember this for exams:

SYSTEM PRIVILEGE
      ↓
WITH ADMIN OPTION

OBJECT PRIVILEGE
      ↓
WITH GRANT OPTION

Example:

GRANT CREATE USER TO hr WITH ADMIN OPTION;

versus:

GRANT SELECT ON emp TO dev_usr WITH GRANT OPTION;
Complete Privilege Flow
                         SYS
                          │
                          │ CREATE USER
                          │ WITH ADMIN OPTION
                          ↓
                         HR
                          │
                          │ CREATE USER
                          ↓
                       DEV_USR
                          │
                          ↓
                    CREATE USER
                          │
                          ↓
                       USERNEW

And object privileges:

GUEST
  │
  └── EMP
       │
       │ GRANT SELECT
       ↓
    DEV_USR
       │
       └── SELECT * FROM GUEST.EMP


DEV_USR
   │
   └── EMP
        │
        │ GRANT SELECT
        ↓
       HR
One Very Important Terminal Rule

Keep checking:

SHOW USER;

before executing a GRANT, CREATE USER, CREATE TABLE, etc.

For this lab:

SYS      → creates tablespaces/users, grants system privileges
GUEST    → creates GUEST.EMP and grants SELECT
DEV_USR  → creates DEV_USR.EMP and grants SELECT
HR       → receives SELECT and later receives/forwards CREATE USER

This one habit would have prevented almost all of the errors you encountered during the lab.
